---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Gallery from '../../components/Gallery.astro';
---

<Layout title="Psychological Manipulation Simulator — Christopher St George">
  <article class="project-page">
    <a href="/" class="back-link">&larr; Back to all projects</a>

    <h1>Psychological Manipulation Simulator</h1>
    <p class="project-meta">
      Producer &amp; Card Artist
    </p>

    <div class="project-body">
      <div class="project-text">
        <section>
          <h2>Overview</h2>
          <p>
            Psychological Manipulation Simulator is an incremental game about
            winning tickets, and multiplying those winnings, all with the aim of
            filling out a massive 250 card collection. Me and a team of 7 other
            students got the opportunity to develop this game from the ground up
            in Unity across two semesters.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>What I did</h2>
          <p>
            Throughout the project, I worked across a few different roles. I
            started as our team's Producer, but with a whopping 52 pieces of
            unique artwork to complete and a chronic lack of artists, I put my art
            hat on at the end of the first semester and began working on creating
            the collectible cards.
          </p>
          <p>
            In addition to the 52 artworks, each card also had 5 rarities, so I
            had to design new card frames for each rarity. Despite being a lot of
            work, creating these assets was incredibly fun. Getting to write the
            story behind each card was really cool and I especially enjoyed the
            moments when I got to tie two cards together and build a kind of
            extended universe with crossovers between cards. On the skills side of
            things, since many of the cards featured similar characters or props,
            I got very, very good at designing assets that could be reused across
            scenes.
          </p>
          <p>
            The technical process for making each card was relatively simple. I
            would model out the card artwork in Blender, export it to Substance
            Painter for texturing, and then render it in Substance Painter. From
            there, I would composite the artwork render with the card frame and
            any accompanying text in Affinity.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>Tools &amp; technologies</h2>
          <ul class="tag-list">
            <li>Substance Painter</li>
            <li>Blender</li>
            <li>Affinity</li>
            <li>Unity</li>
            <li>Figma</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
      </div>

      <div class="project-gallery">
        <!--
          EDIT THIS: once you have real images, put them in public/images/
          and change each "src" below from the placeholder URL to
          something like "/images/project-1-card-1.jpg". Add or remove
          objects from this list for more or fewer photos — the gallery
          adapts automatically.
        -->
        <Gallery
          images={[
            { src: 'https://placehold.co/800x600/1b1e25/9299a6?text=Card+Artwork+1', alt: 'Card artwork 1' },
            { src: 'https://placehold.co/800x600/1b1e25/9299a6?text=Card+Artwork+2', alt: 'Card artwork 2' },
            { src: 'https://placehold.co/800x600/1b1e25/9299a6?text=Card+Artwork+3', alt: 'Card artwork 3' },
          ]}
        />

        <!--
          EDIT THIS: paste your YouTube video's ID below — it's the part
          after "v=" in a normal youtube.com/watch?v=... URL. Then delete
          the placeholder <div> underneath and uncomment this iframe.

          <div class="project-video">
            <iframe
              src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/YOUTUBE_VIDEO_ID"
              title="Project video"
              allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"
              allowfullscreen
            ></iframe>
          </div>
        -->
        <div class="video-placeholder">YouTube video will go here</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
